## std/scoped — Scoped resources (B.8 Phase 3)
##
## Scoped resources are released on block exit, both on normal exit and
## via thrown exceptions. Distinct from `trait Drop` (always-on cleanup
## for owned values): Scoped is for block-leased resources whose release
## may need a parameterized signature like `mutex_unlock(m, value)`.
##
## See `docs/design/SCOPED_RESOURCES.md` for the full design.
##
## Usage (parser + typecheck shipped Phase 2-3; impls ship Phase 5):
##
##   with mutex.lock do v ->
##     # mutex is locked here; v is the protected value
##   end
##   # implicit: mutex_unlock fires on exit (even via throw)
##
##   with file.open(path, "r") do f ->
##     return f.read_all()
##   end
##   # implicit: f.close() on exit
##
## Design decisions captured in the memo:
##   1. Trait-based dispatch (typeclass) — third-party types can be
##      retrofit via separate impl blocks. Java's AutoCloseable cannot.
##   2. Block syntax (extending the existing `with` parser construct)
##      beats function syntax (Scala/Haskell would have shipped this
##      as a free function).
##   3. Distinct from Drop — Drop is owned-value cleanup with a fixed
##      `drop(self): Void` signature; Scoped supports parameterized
##      release via `release(self, value: R): Void`.
##
## Phase 3 ships the trait declaration and typecheck dispatch only.
## No impls; first impl ships in Phase 5 (Mutex). Until then, every
## `with X do x -> body end` form errors with QZ0614 ("X does not
## implement Scoped").

trait Scoped
  ## Acquire the resource. Returns the bound value visible inside the
  ## block via `with X do v -> ... end`. For impls where no useful
  ## bound value exists (e.g., a counter mutex), return 0 and elide
  ## the binding via `with X do -> ... end`.
  def acquire(self): Int

  ## Release on normal block exit.
  def release(self, value: Int): Void

  ## Release on exceptional block exit. Default — same as release.
  ## Override for errdefer-style commit-on-success semantics where
  ## the body's success transfers ownership and only failure releases.
  def release_on_throw(self, value: Int, exc_msg: String): Void
    self.release(value)
  end
end
